        "original_id": "70bbe6df-a7bd-5860-a5c0-6b38214282b6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Ernie Hine, Arthur Chandler, Arthur Lochhead, Johnny Duncan\n- Organizations: Manchester United F.C., Barnsley, Leicester City, Huddersfield Town, The Wednesday\n- Places: Old Trafford, Greater Manchester, England\n- Events: 1928–29 Leicester City F.C. season\n- Topics: English football, club history\n\nFacts:\n- Manchester United Football Club was founded as Newton Heath LYR Football Club in 1878.\n- Manchester United Football Club changed its name to Manchester United in 1902.\n- Ernest William \"Ernie\" Hine was born on 9 April 1901.\n- Manchester United Football Club moved to Old Trafford in 1910.\n- Ernie Hine was a professional footballer who played for Barnsley, Leicester City, Huddersfield Town, and Manchester United.\n- In the 1928–29 season, Leicester City finished runners-up in the First Division, one point behind The Wednesday.\n- In the 1928–29 season, Leicester City's strike force included Ernie Hine, Arthur Chandler, and Arthur Lochhead, with captain Johnny Duncan serving as an inspirational creative leader.\n- Ernie Hine died on 15 April 1974.\n- Manchester United Football Club is based in Old Trafford, Greater Manchester, England, competes in the Premier League, and is nicknamed \"the Red Devils\".",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
